<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shahu of Kolhapur</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1874">1874</a>–<a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Rajarshi Shahu, Shahu Maharaj, Yeshwantrao Ghatge</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shahu of Kolhapur, born Yeshwantrao Ghatge in the family of the chiefs of Kagal, was adopted into the Kolhapur royal house in 1884 and ruled the princely state from 1894 to 1922. A social reformer, he founded schools and hostels, supported the Satyashodhak movement and in 1902 reserved half of government posts for non-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> and backward <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a>, an early form of reservation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shahu-of-kolhapur.jpg" alt="Shahu of Kolhapur" width="320" height="455" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Devare &amp; Co. : Bombay · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maharajah_of_Kolhapur_1912.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Shahu was born Yeshwantrao Ghatge on 26 June 1874, the son of Jaisingrao (Aabasaheb) Ghatge, chief of the Kagal (Sr.) estate and regent of Kolhapur State, and of Radhabai, who came from the royal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghorpade">Ghorpade family</a> of Mudhol. He lost his mother at the age of three. After his adoption into the Kolhapur royal family on 17 March 1884 he studied at Rajkumar College in Rajkot and took lessons in administration from Sir Stuart Fraser of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-civil-service">Indian Civil Service</a>, while a regency council appointed by the British government ran the state.</p><p>In 1891 he married Lakshmibai Khanvilkar, daughter of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> nobleman from Baroda; they had two sons and two daughters. He ascended the throne on 2 April 1894, on coming of age, and was renamed Shahuji Maharaj. Over six feet five inches tall, he was a keen wrestler and patronised the sport throughout his rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> priest of the royal family, Narayan Bhat, refused to perform Vedokta rites for Shahu, implying that he belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shudra">Shudra</a> varna. The Vedokta controversy that followed led Shahu to fight for the rights of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> community. In 1916 he established the Deccan Rayat Association at Nipani to secure political rights for non-Brahmins.</p><p>Besides the reservation order of 26 July 1902, Shahu built hostels for many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> and religious communities, among them Muslims, Jains and Christians, opened Vedic schools to all castes and in 1917 made primary education compulsory and free. His laws sanctioned widow remarriage on 1 January 1917 and inter-caste and inter-religious marriage on 12 July 1919, and the Jogtini Act of 11 November 1920 banned the Devadasi system.</p><p>He opened the Shahu Chhatrapati Spinning and Weaving Mill in 1906, founded co-operative societies and the King Edward Agricultural Institute, and began the Radhanagari Dam on the Bhogawati River in 1907; completed in 1935, it enabled the irrigation of about 15,000 hectares. He built hundreds of taleems, or wrestling training centres, and Khasbag Stadium.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Between 1917 and 1921 Shahu met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar">B. R. Ambedkar</a> a number of times to discuss ways of ending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> segregation. He made Ambedkar chairman of a conference for the untouchables on 21-22 March 1920 and donated Rs. 2,500 when Ambedkar started his newspaper Mooknayak on 31 January 1921. Shahu died in Girgaon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a>, on 6 May 1922 and was succeeded by his eldest son, Rajaram III. In 2006 the Government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> declared his birthday Social Justice Day, and in 2009 a statue of him was unveiled at Parliament House in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-delhi">New Delhi</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shahu of Kolhapur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The family was closely tied to the Kolhapur court, and in 1884 <b>Yeshwantrao Ghatge</b>, son of the chief of Kagal Senior, was adopted into the Kolhapur royal house, becoming the reforming Maharaja Shahu.</blockquote><a href="/state/kagal-senior">Kagal Senior</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.08,72.88&amp;year=1905" aria-label="Mumbai on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6521 1457 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Shahu of Kolhapur"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6971 1727h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6971 1727h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=19.08,72.88&amp;year=1905">What was here in 1905? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
